<Base title="Indexes in PostgreSQL" description="Derive B-tree descent, the planner’s page costs, index-only scans and the visibility map, GIN candidate combinations and BRIN range elimination, then test operator support, physical correlation, HOT updates and maintenance costs.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Inside PostgreSQL</p><h1>Indexes in PostgreSQL</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
 <p>An index keeps information that lets a query avoid examining irrelevant table data. The choice of information determines what the index can prove. Ordered keys can identify a precise interval. Extracted document elements can identify records containing those elements. A minimum and maximum for a group of pages can establish that a requested value is absent from the whole group, while saying little about exactly which records match inside a retained group.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL implements these different approaches as index access methods. We will follow B-tree, GIN and BRIN from the information they store to the work their queries perform. Their value is easier to judge once we can explain why a candidate was found, why it still needs checking, and what a write must maintain.</p>
 <p>The examples use PostgreSQL 18.6 and two browser lessons. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-access-methods">event lesson</a> compares ordered time, JSON containment and page summaries. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-index-candidates">candidate lesson</a> derives array matches and changes physical ordering to weaken BRIN. The server-only skip-scan observation is identified separately.</p>
 <p>This article builds on <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes</a>, which teaches the B-tree, the TID and the choice between an index and a scan across relational databases. Here the subject is how PostgreSQL in particular prices, uses and maintains its indexes.</p>
 <section id="btree"><h2>B-tree indexes and ordered scans</h2>
 <p>A B-tree consists of pages arranged in levels. Internal pages carry boundaries and references to lower pages; leaf pages carry index entries that refer towards heap tuples. Searching a key follows boundaries down to the relevant leaf range. Once there, adjacent ordered entries can satisfy a range request. Links between pages let a scan continue through neighbouring ranges without restarting its search for every row.</p>
 <p>For example, the event lesson inserts 20,000 events, one per second, from 00:00:01 on 1 January 2026. Event 20,000 occurs at 05:33:20. To obtain the newest five, an index ordered by time and then event ID provides an end of the tree from which the executor can read the answer directly.</p>
 <Sql code={ordered} label="Find the newest five through a matching order" />
 <p>The five IDs are 20000 through 19996. The verified plan uses the index without a separate sort and stops after five output rows. If timestamps repeat, event ID supplies a complete tie-breaker. A B-tree can also be scanned backwards; specifying both columns descending here expresses the desired order directly. Mixed ascending and descending requirements need attention to the full key sequence. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-ordering.html">ordering reference</a> explains these alternatives.</p>
 <p>The index has not reordered the heap. Each qualifying index entry still leads towards a heap version when the values or visibility information require a visit. That is why an index route can be poor for a very broad request: ordered keys may identify almost every row, leaving substantial heap work after the index work. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/query-execution/">query-execution experiment</a> shows a large tenant correctly receiving a sequential plan while a small tenant uses the same available index.</p>
<p id="cost">Where does the index route stop paying? The planner knows the table’s size in pages and an estimate of how many rows match. A sequential scan reads every page in order. An index scan reads about one page for each matching row, in the order the index gives, so successive reads land on distant pages. Storage serves neighbouring pages more cheaply than distant ones, so the planner prices the two kinds of read differently. Two settings hold the prices. <code>seq_page_cost</code>, 1 by default, is a page read in sequence, and <code>random_page_cost</code>, 4 by default, is a page fetched out of order. The default of four suited spinning disks, and the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-RANDOM-PAGE-COST">documentation</a> suggests lowering <code>random_page_cost</code> on SSDs or when most of the data is in memory.</p>
<p>The generated table in Indexes shows the prices at work. The planner’s estimate for a sequential scan stays at 2,475 units whatever the range, while its estimate for the bitmap heap scan grows with the number of matching rows: just under 2,475 at <code>customer_id &lt;= 500</code> and just over it at 550, where the plan changed. Lowering <code>random_page_cost</code> makes the index routes cheaper in the estimate and moves that switch towards queries that match more rows. It changes the estimate and not the pages read, so if a change to the setting alters a plan, compare the pages each plan actually read with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> before keeping it.</p>
 <p>Composite keys produce a more specific geometry. In an index on <code>(tier, id)</code>, all entries for one tier form a run, and IDs are ordered inside that run. A condition fixing tier and bounding ID identifies a compact region. With only an ID condition, matching entries may occur in several tier runs. The leading column still determines how the entries are arranged.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL 18 can sometimes use a <strong>skip scan</strong> to make repeated searches for a useful later-column condition. If there are only three tiers, it can seek the requested ID in each tier’s run instead of reading every entry between them. When the leading column has many distinct values, those repeated searches can cost more than another path. This is a costed optimisation, not a promise that leading-key choice no longer matters. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">multicolumn index documentation</a> describes when it is useful.</p>
 <Sql code={skip} label="A later-key equality with three leading-key values" />
 <p>In a separate PostgreSQL 18 server fixture, we inserted 100,000 rows with unique IDs 1–100000 and <code>tier = id % 3</code>, then analysed this index. The query for ID 731 returned one row through an Index Scan reporting four index searches. The extra searches reveal that one statement need not mean one tree descent. This is a measured server example; the browser runtime’s PostgreSQL version may not expose the same optimisation.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="index-only"><h2>Index-only scans and the visibility map</h2>
 <p>An index-only scan answers a query from the index without visiting the heap. It needs every column the query uses to be in the index, and it needs a way to trust each entry. The first is a matter of design: <code>INCLUDE</code> stores extra columns in every entry without making them part of the sort. The history query in Indexes asks for customer 42’s newest orders with their <code>placed_at</code> and <code>total</code> values, so an index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code> that includes <code>total</code> holds everything it needs.</p>
 <p>The second comes from how PostgreSQL keeps row versions. An update writes a new version and keeps the old one for transactions that started earlier, and the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">documentation</a> notes that visibility information is stored in heap entries, not in index entries. So an entry cannot say whether the current transaction may see its row. PostgreSQL keeps one bit per heap page, in the <strong>visibility map</strong>, meaning every row on that page is visible to every transaction. If the bit is set, the scan trusts the entry. If not, it visits the heap to check. <code>VACUUM</code> sets the bits, and any change to a page clears its bit. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#versions">row versions</a> section of the storage article follows those versions.</p>
 <p>Figure 1 shows one leaf page of that index beside the table, using the 36 invented orders from Indexes. Include <code>total</code> first, then run VACUUM, and watch the heap-fetches counter.</p>
 <CoveringFigure number={1} />
 <p>Each ticked box is one page’s bit. With <code>total</code> included but no bits set, the scan still visited each row’s page to check it, and heap fetches stayed at 2. After VACUUM both entries were answered from the index and the count fell to 0.</p>
 <p>The same happens on the generated table from Indexes. The covering index first gave an <code>Index Only Scan</code> with <code>Heap Fetches: 10</code> and 13 pages read, no better than before. The table was freshly loaded, so no bits were set. After <code>VACUUM</code>, it reported <code>Heap Fetches: 0</code> and read 4 pages. The plan node name is therefore not a promise: read <code>Heap Fetches</code> to see how many rows still needed the heap.</p>
 <p>Including <code>total</code> made the index 2.9 MiB instead of 2.3 MiB, beside a 13.5 MiB table. An included column is still part of the index, so changing <code>total</code> now means changing the index too. Bits also come and go on a live table. Autovacuum runs <code>VACUUM</code> on its own schedule and sets bits for pages that have stopped changing, and every write to a page clears its bit until the next vacuum. A covering index pays for a frequent query on a table that changes slowly, and on a busy table <code>Heap Fetches</code> says how much of the benefit remains.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="operators"><h2>Operator classes and supported queries</h2>
 <p>“Index this column” leaves an important question unanswered: how should its values be compared or decomposed? An <strong>operator class</strong> connects an access method to operations and supporting functions for a data type. A B-tree needs consistent ordering. GIN needs to extract keys and decide what combinations can satisfy a query. BRIN needs to build and interpret summaries. The index method supplies a structure; the operator class supplies the relevant meaning of its contents.</p>
 <p>For ordinary integer comparisons, PostgreSQL chooses a suitable default. More specialised expressions require closer attention. A JSON document compared for containment, a text value extracted from that document, and a test for whether a key exists are different operations. They may produce the same answer on today’s records while requiring different index support. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-opclass.html">operator-class reference</a> describes this contract.</p>
 <p>The event lesson makes the difference observable. Every thousandth event has a priority tag, giving twenty matching records. A GIN index with <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> supports the containment query below.</p>
 <Sql code={json} label="Use an operator supported by the document index" />
 <p>Change the predicate to <code>attributes -&gt;&gt; 'tag' = 'priority'</code>. The lesson still returns twenty records, but the plan scans the table: it is now comparing an extracted text expression, rather than using whole-document containment. A B-tree expression index on that exact extraction would be a different design. A query rewrite is appropriate only when its meaning is correct for all permitted records, not merely when the current fixture happens to produce the same IDs.</p>
 <p>Likewise, <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> and the default <code>jsonb_ops</code> store different searchable information and support different operators. Path-and-value hashes make the former focused for its supported containment and path searches; it does not support the top-level key-existence operator <code>?</code>. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/json/">JSON chapter</a> derives these choices from document structure and the exact expression being queried.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="gin"><h2>GIN indexes and posting lists</h2>
 <p>A single array or document can contain several searchable elements. GIN, an inverted index, extracts keys from each value and associates each key with the heap references where it occurs. A key with relatively few references can keep a compact posting list; a frequently occurring key can need a posting tree. Searching starts from the requested keys rather than inspecting every complete document.</p>
 <GinCandidates />
 <p>In the drawing, the audit list contains A and C, while urgent contains B and C. “Contains both” intersects the lists and retains C. “Contains either” combines them and retains A, B and C. This reasoning scales to longer lists, although the real implementation uses compressed references and supporting structures. GIN’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/gin.html#GIN-INTRODUCTION">introduction</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/gin.html#GIN-IMPLEMENTATION">implementation description</a> explain the extracted-key and posting representation.</p>
 <p>The candidate lesson creates 20,000 documents. Every hundredth ID contains audit, giving 200. Every 250th contains urgent, giving 80. IDs divisible by 500 contain both, giving 40. We can therefore predict both queries before running them.</p>
 <Sql code={gin} label="Intersect or combine extracted array elements" />
 <p>The containment query returns 40. The overlap query returns 240: 200 plus 80, minus the 40 counted twice. Both verified plans use the GIN index to collect candidate locations and a Bitmap Heap Scan to visit their heap pages. The bitmap arranges heap work by location; it does not preserve an application ordering such as newest-first. Adding a timestamp sort still asks for separate work unless another chosen path supplies that order.</p>
 <p>Candidate precision depends on the operator class and the representation used. For these ordinary array membership conditions, exact element references can establish the membership condition. Heap visibility still needs attention. If the bitmap becomes lossy under its memory constraints, it retains whole candidate pages instead of every exact tuple location, so rows on those pages need the original condition rechecked. Other GIN operator classes can require rechecks for their own reasons, such as information lost by hashing or by an incomplete representation of the original value.</p>
 <p>A displayed <code>Recheck Cond</code> therefore tells you the condition available for checking; it does not prove that every candidate was a false positive. Read the actual heap-block precision and rows removed by recheck. The distinction is useful when an index finds a small exact set in one run but a much broader set of pages under different memory or query conditions.</p>
 <p>Change the operator from contains-both to overlaps and predict the extra work. There are six times as many matching records in this fixture. If the application needs the newest ten matches, it may now have six times as many candidates to order even though the final limit remains ten. An index match is the beginning of that plan, not its complete cost.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="brin"><h2>BRIN indexes and page-range summaries</h2>
 <p>BRIN stores summaries for ranges of physical heap pages. For a timestamp column using a min/max operator class, a summary records bounds. If a requested minute lies outside those bounds, no row in the range can match and its pages can be skipped. If it lies inside, the range remains a candidate. The summary does not say that the minute is actually present, or which rows contain it.</p>
 <p>Consider two tables containing exactly the same 20,000 timestamped events. In the first, insertion follows timestamp order. In the second, insertion follows a deterministic permutation of event IDs. The values and query answer are identical; their placement on heap pages differs. Each table gets a BRIN index with four pages per range, a small setting chosen to expose the effect in a teaching fixture.</p>
 <Sql code={brin} label="Ask the ordered table for one minute" />
 <p>The query includes seconds 1800 through 1859, so it must return sixty events. In the observed ordered table, the first four pages held timestamps from 00:00:01 through 00:02:40. The next four held 00:02:41 through 00:05:20. Neither could contain the requested half-hour minute. Compact bounds allow whole stretches of the heap to be excluded.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed bounds from the same records in different heap orders"><table><thead><tr><th>Physical placement</th><th>Pages 0–3: earliest</th><th>Pages 0–3: latest</th><th>Could contain 00:30?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timestamp order</td><td>00:00:01</td><td>00:02:40</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Permuted order</td><td>00:00:48</td><td>05:33:20</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The second table’s first range spans almost the whole data interval. Many later ranges do too. These broad summaries cannot rule out much for the same one-minute query. Our server chose a BRIN bitmap path for the ordered table and a sequential scan for the scattered one. Both returned sixty. We did not disable sequential scans to make the second index appear useful.</p>
 <p>For the useful BRIN path, the Bitmap Heap Scan reports lossy blocks because the summary identifies page ranges, not exact matching tuple positions. PostgreSQL visits those pages and checks the timestamps. Some candidate rows belong to adjacent minutes and are rejected. This extra checking is the price of keeping much less information than a per-row timestamp index.</p>
 <p>Changing <code>pages_per_range</code> adjusts that tradeoff. Smaller ranges keep more summaries and may rule out more pages; larger ranges are more compact but blend more values together. Neither setting repairs a heap whose values are broadly scattered within every range. PostgreSQL also offers other BRIN operator classes with different summary representations; the simple bounds here are the usual min/max model, not the only possible BRIN representation.</p>
 <p>New ranges need summarisation, and summaries must remain conservative as values change. An unsummarised range cannot safely be excluded. Summarisation functions, vacuum and the optional autosummarize behaviour provide maintenance paths. Consult the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/brin.html">BRIN reference</a> when operating an append-heavy table. Late arrivals can widen bounds even when most inserts remain time-ordered.</p>
 <p>BRIN neither rearranges the heap nor creates separately managed partitions. It describes existing physical ranges. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-index-candidates">candidate lesson</a> lets you compare the two placements and widen the query to a full day. When every record belongs to that day, there is no unrelated time range for either BRIN index to exclude.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="writes"><h2>Index maintenance and write costs</h2>
 <p>Each structure creates work on writes. A B-tree insertion must find the correct leaf and make space for the new entry. If the page cannot fit it, PostgreSQL first tries to make room by removing entries for row versions no transaction can see any more, or by merging duplicate keys into a posting list. Only if that fails does a split move entries to a new page and add a parent reference; that parent can itself need a split. The tree stays shallow by branching widely, but maintaining its order is still real work. Updates that cannot use the heap-only shortcut can create new entries referring to new row versions. See the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html#BTREE-IMPLEMENTATION">B-tree implementation</a> and the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">row-versions investigation</a>.</p>
 <h3 id="hot">Heap-only tuple updates</h3>
 <p>An update writes a new row version. If no indexed column changed and the new version fits on the same page, the update is a <strong>heap-only tuple</strong> (HOT) update, and no index is touched: the old entries still lead to the page, and the old version leads on to the new one. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-hot.html">documentation</a> describes the mechanism, and the storage article’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/#hot">VACUUM and HOT updates</a> section shows how the old entries keep working. An <code>INCLUDE</code> column counts as indexed, so changing one rules out a HOT update. A column used only by a BRIN index does not: since PostgreSQL 16, an update that changes only BRIN-indexed columns can still be HOT, because a BRIN summary holds no per-row entries. That is the write cost of the covering index above.</p>
 <p>In a separate check on an ordinary table built like the generated one from Indexes, updating <code>total</code> on 600 rows gave 600 HOT updates when no index held <code>total</code>, and none once the covering index included it. That table was created with <code>fillfactor = 90</code>, which leaves a tenth of each page free. With the default fillfactor of 100, the freshly loaded pages had no room, and none of the 600 updates was HOT. HOT needs space on the page as well as an unindexed column.</p>
 <h3>Maintenance in GIN and BRIN</h3>
 <p>A GIN document can contribute many keys. Updating that document can therefore require more than maintaining one index entry. With fast update enabled, PostgreSQL can accumulate insertions in a pending list and merge them into the main structure later. Reads must account for pending entries too, and cleanup can make write latency uneven. An index built once over static data does not expose the same maintenance pattern as a sustained stream of changing documents.</p>
 <p>A BRIN summary is compact because it represents many rows together. Its ongoing usefulness depends on its ranges remaining selective and being summarised. Small index size alone is not sufficient: the scattered-table experiment has a small summary structure that excludes too little to justify using it for the selected query.</p>
 <h3 id="concurrently">Building an index while writes continue</h3>
 <p>A plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes to the table until it finishes, because rows changed during the build would otherwise be missing from the index. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY"><code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code></a> lets writes continue, and the price follows from that. A scan of the table sees only the rows that existed when it began, so the build needs a second scan to catch up with the rows written during the first, and it must wait for transactions that could still change or read the table. It does more work and takes longer. If the build fails part way, it leaves an invalid index behind, which queries ignore but writes still maintain. Drop it, or rebuild it with <code>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>. Because it runs as several transactions, it cannot run inside a transaction block, which matters to migration tools that wrap every change in one.</p>
 <h3>Choosing among access methods</h3>
 <p>The decision is consequently more specific than choosing a favourite access method. Write the exact query, derive the information that could eliminate work, and inspect a representative plan with actual candidates, rechecks, heap visits and ordering. Then include the index’s storage, build procedure and maintenance under the expected write rate. For specialised range, spatial or nearest-neighbour operators, start with the supporting type and operator class; the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/extensions/">types and extensions chapter</a> follows that connection.</p>
 </section>
